---
// Port of about.markdown (body inlined as HTML; the page has no dynamic
// data). Note: this body was previously passed to `render()` as a raw
// markdown string, but this Astro version's render() only accepts content
// entries, so the page silently rendered empty. Hand-written HTML avoids
// the question. The old index.markdown intro (mission statement) moved here
// as "About this project" when the home page became the update feed.
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const OKLO_NEWSROOM_URL =
  'https://oklo.com/newsroom/news-details/2026/Oklos-Atomic-Alchemy-Announces-U-S--Department-of-Energy-Approval-for-Nuclear-Safety-Design-Agreement-of-its-Groves-Isotopes-Test-Reactor/default.aspx';
const NPR_URL = 'https://www.npr.org/2026/01/28/nx-s1-5677187/nuclear-safety-rules-rewritten-trump';
const FACEBOOK_URL = 'https://www.facebook.com/groups/987252890512778';
const CONTACT_EMAIL = 'contact@lockhartnuke.watch';
---

<BaseLayout title="About">
  <article>
    <h1>About</h1>
    <h2>About this project</h2>
    <p>
      "Lockhart Nuke Watch" was started to try and gather information about
      the Groves Isotope nuclear reactor being built in Lockhart, Texas by
      Oklo.
    </p>
    <p>
      The
      <a href={OKLO_NEWSROOM_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener"
        >Groves radioactive isotope facility being built by Oklo in Lockhart, Texas</a
      >
      has received very little publicity until now. When starting this
      research, the only available materials were slick press releases and
      almost impenetrable regulatory documents.
    </p>
    <p>
      This site seeks to correct that and raise awareness of a consequential
      decision that is being made for the people and living creatures of
      Lockhart, Texas and the surrounding Central Texas area, whether they
      are aware or not.
    </p>
    <p>
      Projects like Groves are being fast tracked
      <a href={NPR_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener"
        >under sweeping changes made to nuclear safety directives under the
        Trump administration</a
      >
      starting in 2025. This is not a political issue, but a safety issue.
      Read the previous link, review the changes in the wording, the
      side-by-side before and after changes. Ask yourself why the language
      has been so softened and compromised.
    </p>
    <p>
      Keep these changes in mind when reviewing the questions and their
      researched context below. Operating a nuclear reactor, even a small
      one, is a serious undertaking that demands transparency and
      accountability to the lives of the people depending on its responsible
      design and stewardship.
    </p>
    <p>
      There are many questions about this project, based on research
      conducted in pursuit of what that should look like, and how to even
      know how to ask for it. So many, that they are grouped according to
      topic in the sidebar.
    </p>
    <p>
      As a central Texans, these are the kinds of questions we should be
      asking, for our future, our children and grandchildren's futures.
    </p>
    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>
      Find us at
      <a href={FACEBOOK_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener"
        >Lockhart Nuke Watch on Facebook</a
      >
      or <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      What else do you wish you knew about this Lockhart nuclear reactor?
      Email: <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a> with
      your questions and concerns. All we have is each other.
    </p>
    <h2>About the author</h2>
    <p>Well, it ain't about me, darlin'.</p>
    <p>
      When I (the author) discovered this project, Groves seemed so far
      along in fast-tracked approval, while being simultaneously difficult
      to learn anything substantial about it other than from press releases.
      I wanted to try and distill the research I was doing, to protect the
      people I love and the area I grew up in.
    </p>
    <p>
      I'm not a reporter, but maybe a reporter can find it useful. If you
      have feedback from this perspective, I would readily receive it. Never
      done anything like this before.
    </p>
    <p>
      I'm not a nuclear engineer by trade, but I am somewhat technical. You
      might say that I am a humble permanent student of complex systems. I
      would welcome review and feedback from experts in the regulatory and
      engineering domains that intersect with this issue.
    </p>
    <p>
      I learned about Groves in passing conversation, someone from out of
      town shooting their mouth off. They were bragging about how easy it is
      to do anything in Texas, to Texas, because there's no protections
      here. And when I looked into it... I was shocked.
    </p>
    <p>Don't mess with Texas.</p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
